  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-2ee14edd Web logs overview\"**\n- ID: `eff-2ee14edd-web-logs-overview-e70f73c03a89`\n- Six panels: total requests, unique client IPs, total bytes, requests over time by status code, requests by status code, and top countries by requests.\n- Time range is set to 2026-09-27 through 2026-11-27 because the sample data sits in that window. Today is 2026-10-10, so a relative range like \"last 7 days\" would show little or nothing.\n\n**Rule: \"EFF-2ee14edd 5xx spike\"**\n- ID: `2bfe8cdc-a8ae-4cca-a777-81143c08c815`\n- Type `.es-query` on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`.\n- Matches `response.keyword` wildcard `5*`, counts over a 5-minute window, and fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Runs every 1m and is enabled. It has no action connector, so it will show firings in the alerts list but will not notify anyone.\n\n**Data findings:**\n- The data has 14,074 documents with three response codes: 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441). The only 5xx traffic is the 503s.\n- Top countries are CN, IN, and US. Top extensions are gz, css, and zip.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- Tagged the rule with `EFF-2ee14edd`.\n- Enabled the rule, since you asked for it to check every minute.\n- Reached Elasticsearch directly on port 19200 for the exploration queries, because the Kibana console proxy is disabled on this instance.\n\nThe rule's status is still `pending` on the first read, so I have not confirmed it has run a check yet.",
